e: Owner concern (verbatim in brief): \"tests taking hours... this is our bottleneck... maybe a testing policy so that only tests that are relevant for the change needs to be tested\". Dispatched kimi-k3.1-pro (owner-named model) via the kimi CLI with a read-only agent file (first kimi dispatch; --agent-file /tmp/kimi-readonly-agent.md, session ID from ~/.kimi-code/session_index.jsonl per the reference). Brief collab/1793209293822-kimi-test-strategy-investigation.prompt.md: current strategy (Gate Ladder, tiers, test:changed/affected.clj), measured baselines, gap analysis (observed: fresh-worktree rebuild costs, unsharded laptop tier ~100min vs 5min sharded, the clj -M:test slow-tier footgun hit twice), three proposal tiers (POLICY change-class->gate table; MECHANICS laptop sharding/worktree pool/remote-first; STRUCTURAL tagging/runner), one highest-leverage recommendation. Constraint stated in brief: tri-host gates and landing rules are not negotiable away.\norchestrator-logs/1793192500000-glm-resume-yang-clojure-hardening.md:91:Done: After my three failed kimi dispatches, the owner pointed me back to delegate-invocation-reference.md; re-reading showed my deviations: --output-format text (recipe: stream-json), agent-file+session combination (reference: bind at creation, never on resume), -m on --resume (wrong), --auto with -p (forbidden), and a wrong root-cause hypothesis (the \"early exits\" were likely text-mode stream misreads). Re-dispatched EXACTLY per the canonical recipe (fresh session, stream-json): SUCCESS — 38 tool events, full five-section report (12KB, collab/1793214000000-kimi-test-strategy-investigation-r2.kimi-k3.1-pro.findings.md), session session_b1a13f11-3550-4a81-9a33-ce657a538a31 (captured from ~/.kimi-code/session_index.jsonl). Findings highlight: the selection mechanism is NOT the bottleneck (test:changed:list on yang.clojure.cljc = 1.26s, 61/327 namespaces) — EXECUTION is (unsharded JVM changed lane timed out at 300s); cold prerequisites regenerate ANTLR 3x; wide-change exit-2 is not fail-closed as a workflow. Recommendations: (policy) explicit change-class->gate routing table incl. automatic G3 on wide changes; (mechanics) make SHARDED quick verification the default G2/G3 path (4-6 JVM workers on laptop, remote for lanes predicted >15min, content-keyed artifact caching, warm worktree pool); (structure) retime before re-tiering, medium tier, split mega-suites by contract. Highest-leverage: sharded-by-default G2/G3 execution with automatic wide-change dispatch. Full measured baselines incl. history mining from the old orchestrator log per owner instruction.\norchestrator-logs/1793192500000-glm-resume-yang-clojure-hardening.md:212:Done: Owner: \"this track is complete?\" -> answered no (slice-6 lanes pending + slice 7 not dispatched) and, per the standing don't-stay-idle directive, dispatched slice 7 in parallel (worktree yang-slice7 off 8125a1bb). Owner asked \"can slice 7 start without 6?\" -> answered yes: the library needs only slices 4+5 (both in base), written with if/let/do/loop-recur; the only slice-6-dependent deliverable is the per-arm case fixture derivation, which defers to a follow-up if slice 6's lanes surprise us (base contains slice 6 anyway). Brief (collab/1793260976007-*): the guest collection/higher-order library per the plan's facility table (all guest-side, outcome-model aware, reduced supported), apply over both conventions, TOTAL scaffold retirement (grep-proven), per-arm case fixtures, the regeneration dev script, anonymous-fn arity markers, profile-derived adapter caps.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
