omain Code=5 \\\\\\\"Input/output error\\\\\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\\\n4d9a84a8 fix(dev): sub:list headers carry counts — the cljs/cljd section match missed every namespace\\\\n815b7823 fix(dev): node_shard's :env uses :extra-env too\\\\na601f144 fix(dev): :env replaces the child environment; use :extra-env so mise finds HOME\\\\nf6d16921 Merge origin/master\\\\n4412f955 fix(dev): the sharded lane drivers exec nothing when :err :append is set\\\\n4ddf9a69 docs(orchestrator-logs): all work stopped until further notice (owner directive)\\\\neec49f7a fix(dev): node_shard runs npm ci through mise exec\\\\n78505d18 fix(dev): node_shard runs npm ci only when npm exists (bare-node hosts use the persistent node_modules)\\\\n516f9594 feat(dev): subsystem-sharded Node and Dart lane drivers in babashka\\\\n183a8481 docs(orchestrator-logs): the yang-python retake seat's one-record adoption entry\\\\n589aeb83 Merge origin/master: the nightly-red round landing and collab rulings\\\\ndeaa290c docs(roles): the collab visibility split — the committed subfolders are on master, the in-flight root is per-checkout scratch\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ncollab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:22:   - **The nightly JVM lane excludes slow tests.** `jupiter:~/ci/shard-jvm.sh:30` invokes `clojure -M:test -e :slow`. `nightly-jvm.sh:55` calls this script and never calls `slow-tier.sh`. Consequently, the deployed nightly is not the stated full JVM suite.\\ncollab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:64:   `shiva:~/ci/nightly-full.sh:33` writes a persistent SHA-only file. `jupiter:~/ci/nightly-jvm.sh:35–45` accepts the first readable value whose object exists locally. There is no run date, generation identifier, or freshness check.\\ncollab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:75:   **Yes: an unpinned run should be advisory for the combined matrix.** Its individual lane remains useful evidence for its actual SHA, but it cannot complete another SHA’s matrix.\\ncollab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:77:   **Required:** atomically publish `{run-id, full-SHA, timestamp}`; require the expected Vietnam nightly date; validate the commit; check every Git command and actual `HEAD`; join results only by matching run-id and SHA. Record fallback reasons explicitly.\\ncollab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:98:   - **Logs are overwritten:** all shard scripts delete their fixed `target-ci/*shards` directory at startup. Jupiter’s timestamped nightly log captures summary output, while individual test stdout remains in the directory deleted next run (`nightly-jvm.sh:55`, `shard-jvm.sh:9,30`).\\ncollab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:99:   - **Attribution is incomplete:** the summaries lack a consistent full-SHA/run-id/host/toolchain manifest. Pulling `nightly-jvm-latest-summary.txt` can attach a previous run’s result (`nightly-full.sh:48`).\\ncollab/1793465000000-engineer-nightly-red-fix.codex.stdout.log:5:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793456746000-orchestrator-yangpython-nightly-red.prompt.md && cat docs/design/datom.world.md && git status --short --branch && git branch --show-current && git rev-parse HEAD'\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"Created-GMT: 2026-10-31 14:25:46 GMT\\\\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-31 21:25:55 +07\\\\nCoding-Agent: interactive\\\\nSession-ID: sess_b6001731-427a-4431-b74f-97f348558fc2\\\\n\\\\n# Task: investigate and close the yang.python JVM nightly red (3 failures, cross-host confirmed)\\\\n\\\\nRole: yang.python Orchestrator (peer seat)\\\\n\\\\nImplementers:\\\\n- Model: <yang.python seat's current orchestrator model> | Assigned: 2026-10-31 21:25 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: the yang.python track owns the red shard\\\\n\\\\n## Evidence (verified before this brief)\\\\n\\\\nThe first nightly run (new infra, stood up 2026-10-31 ~02:23 by another seat) ran the JVM\\\\nquick tier sharded at commit `c02c7c5fa` on BOTH hosts. Result: 13 of 14 shards green;\\\\n`yang.python` rc=1 on BOTH — same failures, so this is a master regression, not host flake.\\\\n\\\\n- shiva: `~/ci/nightly-results/20261031-0223-summary.txt` (jvm-sharded lanes=14 red-lanes=1\\\\n  secs=1325; yang.python rc=1, 265 tests / 2,875 assertions). NOTE: shiva's nightly kept only\\\\n  per-lane summary lines, not per-shard stdout — repro from jupiter's artifacts (next).\\\\n- jupiter: `~/ci/datomworld-nightly/target-ci/shards/yang.python.log` — full per-shard stdout.\\\\n  Exactly 3 failures:\\\\n\\\\n1. `ERROR in (an-unsupplied-import-is-refused-at-publication-test)`\\\\n   (`test/yang/python/antlr/dht_import_test.cljc:107`)\\\\n   expected: `{:status :refused, :reason :yang.python.antlr/unpublished-import, :module \\\\\\\"k\\\\\\\",\\\\n   :imported-by \\\\\\\"t\\\\\\\"}` from `publication/publish-units!`\\\\n   actual: `clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo: dao.jing reads only valid segment content addresses\\\\n   {:address :test/address}` at `dao.jing.cljc:445`, through\\\\n   `yang.python.antlr.publication/publish-units!` (publication.cljc:76).\\\\n   The test stubs a placeholder address `:test/address`; dao.jing now refuses it.\\\\n\\\\n2. `FAIL in (module-uast-is-one-wide-application-test)` (`linked_prelude_test.cljc:103`)\\\\n3. `FAIL in (module-uast-is-one-wide-application-test)` (`linked_prelude_test.cljc:112`)\\\\n\\\\nAll three sit in the `yang.python.antlr.dht-import-test` and `linked-prelude` namespaces.\\\\n\\\\n## What to do\\\\n\\\\n1. Re-derive, do not trust: read `git log` around `c02c7c5fa` and the diff that introduced\\\\n   dao.jing's segment-address validation (the \\\\\\\"hash-registry contract\\\\\\\" work landed a lint and\\\\n   a stricter `dao.jing` read path — suspect, not conclusion). Confirm which commit made\\\\n   `dao.jing` refuse `:test/address`.\\\\n2. Adjudicate the defect's location:\\\\n   - If `publish-units!` should never pass an unhashable placeholder (the test fixture is\\\\n     stale — it predates the validation), fix the yang.python test fixture to use a real\\\\n     content address while still exercising the `:unpublished-import` refusal path.\\\\n   - If dao.jing's validation is wrong to fire on this shape, the defect is NOT yours: file\\\\n     it to the dao.jing owner seat with the evidence and keep this round scoped to what\\\\n     yang.python owns. Do not change dao.jing semantics from the yang.python seat.\\\\n   - For `module-uast-is-one-wide-application-test`: diagnose before touching; determine\\\\n     whether it is the same root cause (fixture addresses) or an independent break.\\\\n3. Fix in a yang.python worktree per the standard cycle: G0 iterate\\\\n   (`bb test:sub:clj yang.python`), G1 (`bb test:sub yang.python`), then\\\\n   `bb test:changed` tri-host. The regression is JVM-visible; still verify Node/Dart legs\\\\n   before landing because the same fixtures run there (the cljs tier showed a large\\\\n   `dht_import_test` failure cluster on shiva's 20261031-0223 run — likely the same root,\\\\n   confirm while you are in there).\\\\n4. Land through your normal gate + independent review; commit message names the fixture or\\\\n   code defect, whichever it is, and cites this brief.\\\\n\\\\n## Constraints\\\\n\\\\n- Scope: yang.python test/publication code and fixtures. dao.jing changes route to the\\\\n  dao.jing seat. No new public entry points.\\\\n- Routing (routing-status.md, 2026-10-31 relay): codex, glm, agy, deepseek, vibe available;\\\\n  claude/cmd unavailable; muse geo-blocked. Reviewer must be a different model family from\\\\n  the implementer.\\\\n- Collab filing (orchestrator.md step 11, 2026-10-31 ruling): your round's artifacts live\\\\n  flat in `collab/` while in flight; file them into `collab/yang-python/` when the round\\\\n  closes, checking existing subfolders first.\\\\n\\\\n## Deliverables\\\\n\\\\n- Findings: `collab/<timestamp>-orchestrator-yangpython-nightly-red.findings.md` — root cause\\\\n  per failure (or the filed handoff to dao.jing), the fix diff, verification counts\\\\n  (JVM/Node/Dart), and the review round reference.\\\\n- A one-line nightly hygiene recommendation back to the owner: shiva's nightly-full.sh\\\\n  discarded per-shard stdout (only summary lines kept) — the diagnosis above was only\\\\n  possible from jupiter's copy; both hosts should keep per-shard logs.\\\\n---\\\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\\\n\\\\n## Core Philosophy\\\\n\\\\nEverything is data.\\\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\\\n\\\\n### Foundational Axioms\\\\n\\\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\\\n   At system scale, a \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\\\n\\\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\\\n\\\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\\\n\\\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\\\n\\\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\\\n\\\\n## Streams\\\\n\\\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\\\n\\\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\\\nthe stream's own.\\\\n\\\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\\\n\\\\n### Host Boundaries\\\\n\\\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\\\n\\\\n## Architectural Commitments\\\\n\\\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\\\n\\\\n## Design Principles\\\\n\\\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\\\n\\\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\\\n\\\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\\\n  ordinary streams.\\\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\\\n  auto-index observer.\\\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\\\n\\\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\\\nretired `:global` tag in\\\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\\\n\\\\n## Code Style\\\\n\\\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\\\n\\\\n## Development Workflow\\\\n\\\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\\\n  leaves to review.\\\\n\\\\n### Problem Decomposition\\\\n\\\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\\\n2. Identify invariants.\\\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\\\n\\\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\\\n\\\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\\\n## nightly-red-fix\\\\n M test/yang/python/antlr/dht_import_test.cljc\\\\n M test/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc\\\\n?? collab/1793465000000-engineer-nightly-red-fix.prompt.md\\\\nnightly-red-fix\\\\nc02c7c5fa4393f6462097140380cd39af2452a96\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ncollab/1793465000000-engineer-nightly-red-fix.codex.stdout.log:7:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md'\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"---\\\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# BUILD & TEST\\\\n\\\\n## Commands\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\\\\\"Remote test servers\\\\\\\" below for\\\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\\\n#\\\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\\\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\\\\\"Remote test servers\\\\\\\" below).\\\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\\\n\\\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\\\n\\\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\\\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\\\\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\\\\n\\\\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\\\\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\\\\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\\\\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\\\\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\\\\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\\\\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\\\\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\\\\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\\\\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\\\\n\\\\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\\\\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\\\\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\\\\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\\\\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\\\\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\\\\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\\\\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\\\\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\\\\n\\\\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\\\\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\\\\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\\\\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\\\\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\\\\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\\\\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\\\\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\\\\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\\\\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\\\\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\\\\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\\\\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\\\\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\\\\n\\\\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\\\\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\\\\n\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\\\\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\\\\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\\\\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\\\\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\\\\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\\\\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n\\\\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\\\\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\\\\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\\\\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\\\\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\\\\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\\\\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\\\\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\\\\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\\\\n\\\\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\\\\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\\\\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\\\\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\\\\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\\\\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\\\\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\\\\n\\\\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\\\\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\\\\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\\\\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\\\\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\\\\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\\\\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\\\\navailable. Do not run another lane set concurrently on the laptop.\\\\n\\\\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\\\\n\\\\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\\\\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\\\\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\\\\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\\\\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\\\\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\\\\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\\\\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\\\\n\\\\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\\\\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\\\\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\\\\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\\\\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\\\\n\\\\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\\\\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\\\\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\\\\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\\\\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\\\\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\\\\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\\\\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\\\\n\\\\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\\\\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\\\\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\\\\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\\\\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\\\\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\\\\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\\\\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\\\\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\\\\n\\\\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\\\\n\\\\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\\\\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\\\\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\\\\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\\\\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\\\\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\\\\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\\\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\\\\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\\\\n\\\\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\\\\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\\\\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\\\\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\\\\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\\\\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\\\\n\\\\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\\\\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\\\\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\\\\n\\\\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\\\\n\\\\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\\\\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\\\\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\\\\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\\\\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \\\\\\\"Test tiers: quick and slow\\\\\\\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\\\\nslice L5) is two tests:\\\\n\\\\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\\\\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\\\\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\\\\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\\\\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\\\\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\\\\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\\\\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\\\\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\\\\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\\\\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\\\\n  REPL.\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\\\\n\\\\n# ClojureDart\\\\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\\\n\\\\n# Linting\\\\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\\\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\\\\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\\\\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\\\\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\\\\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\\\\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\\\\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\\\\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\\\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\\\\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\\\\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\\\\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\\\\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\\\\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\\\\n\\\\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\\\\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\\\\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\\\\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\\\\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\\\\n\\\\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\\\\n\\\\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\\\\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\\\\n\\\\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\\\\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\\\\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\\\\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\\\\\\\"When to tag a test slow\\\\\\\"): a deftest that\\\\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\\\\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\\\\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\\\\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\\\\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\\\\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\\\\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\\\\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\\\\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\\\\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\\\\n\\\\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \\\\\\\"Remote test\\\\nservers\\\\\\\" for how to run them):\\\\n\\\\n| Run | Time |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\\\\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\\\\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\\\\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\\\\n\\\\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\\\\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\\\\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\\\\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\\\\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\\\\n\\\\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\\\\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\\\\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\\\\\\\"test\\\\\\\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\\\\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\\\\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\\\\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\\\\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\\\\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\\\\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\\\\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\\\\nset -u\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\"\\\\ncd \\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\nPAR=\\\\\\\"${2:-8}\\\\\\\"\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\neval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\"\\\\nOUT=\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci/shards\\\\\\\"; rm -rf \\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\"\\\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\\\necho \\\\\\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\\\\\" | tee \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\\\\\"$OUT/npm.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1                   && echo \\\\\\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\\\\\"$OUT/build-node.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1     && echo \\\\\\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\\\\\"$OUT/antlr.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1             && echo \\\\\\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nSUBS=\\\\\\\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\\\\\\\"\\\\n: > \\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nfor s in $SUBS; do\\\\n  bb test:sub:list \\\\\\\"$s\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null | awk \\\\\\\"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\");print}\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n  cat \\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\"$s $(wc -l < \\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\"$OUT/plan.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\ndone\\\\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\\\\nfind test -type f \\\\\\\\( -name \\\\\\\"*_test.clj\\\\\\\" -o -name \\\\\\\"*_test.cljc\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\) | sed -e \\\\\\\"s#^test/##\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\"s#\\\\\\\\.cljc\\\\\\\\?\\\\\\\\$##\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\"s#/#.#g\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\"s#_#-#g\\\\\\\" | sort -u > \\\\\\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nsort -u \\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\\\\\"\\\\ncomm -23 \\\\\\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\necho \\\\\\\"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nrun_shard() {\\\\n  s=$1; f=\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\"; [ -s \\\\\\\"$f\\\\\\\" ] || return 0\\\\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \\\\\\\"$n\\\\\\\"); done < \\\\\\\"$f\\\\\\\"\\\\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \\\\\\\"${args[@]}\\\\\\\" >\\\\\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \\\\\\\"^Ran \\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\\\\\" | tail -1)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n}\\\\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\\\\nprintf \\\\\\\"%s\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \\\\\\\"$PAR\\\\\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\\\\\"run_shard {}\\\\\\\"\\\\necho \\\\\\\"done $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\\\\nset -u\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\"\\\\ncd \\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\nPAR=\\\\\\\"${2:-6}\\\\\\\"\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\neval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\"\\\\nOUT=\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\\\\\\\"; rm -rf \\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\"\\\\nrun_ns() {\\\\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\\\\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \\\\\\\"$n\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \\\\\\\"^Ran \\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\\\\\" | tail -1)\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n}\\\\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\\\\necho \\\\\\\"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nxargs -P \\\\\\\"$PAR\\\\\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\\\\\"run_ns {}\\\\\\\" < \\\\\\\"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\necho \\\\\\\"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\\\\n\\\\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\\\\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\\\\nand `git`.\\\\n\\\\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\\\\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot (rechecked 2026-10-29): java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.8 (the stale hand-symlinked 3.41.9 was replaced via `mise install flutter@3.47.8` after it failed to compile `dart:ui`-using cljd namespaces; if the Dart lane dies in the cljd compile, check `mise ls flutter` first). Tri-host quick tier verified green on 2026-10-29 (see Measured times). |\\\\n\\\\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\\\\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\\\\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\\\\n\\\\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\\\\n\\\\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\\\\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\\\\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\\\\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\\\\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\\\\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\\\\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\\\\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\\\\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \\\\\\\"$URL\\\\\\\";\\\\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\\\\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\\\\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\\\\n\\\\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\\\\n\\\\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\\\\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\\\\ninto the clone:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\\\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\\\\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\\\\neval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\\\\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\\\\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \\\\\\\"Can't\\\\nfind flutter on PATH\\\\\\\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\\\\n\\\\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\\\\n\\\\n\\\\\\\"One lane set at a time\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\"one Dart lane at a time\\\\\\\" are per-machine rules about\\\\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\\\\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\\\\\\\"on one machine where\\\\navoidable\\\\\\\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\\\\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\\\\n\\\\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\\\\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\\\\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\\\\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\\\\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\\\\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\\\\n  by the design's own reasoning (\\\\\\\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\\\\\\\"), not yet\\\\n  verified by a measured run.\\\\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\\\\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\\\\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\\\\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\\\\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\\\\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\\\\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\\\\n\\\\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\\\\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\\\\nseparate Dart clone.\\\\n\\\\n### The jolt J2 lane: run it with `--jobs` on the servers\\\\n\\\\nThe jolt lane's core mode (`bb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core [--jobs N]\\\\n[--only re] [--timeout-ms M]`) runs each test namespace as its own child jolt\\\\nprocess (own Chez VM, own native parser library load, own log under\\\\n`build/j2-logs/core/`), so the namespaces cannot corrupt one another — the\\\\nonly thing `--jobs N` overlaps is the waiting. The default is `jobs 1`\\\\n(strictly serial), which on jupiter makes the full ~179-namespace lane a\\\\n5-6-hour run.\\\\n\\\\nOn the servers, run it with `--jobs 16`:\\\\n\\\\n- jolt children are essentially single-threaded (the 16 MiB parse thread\\\\n  only burns during parses) and cost 1-2 GB each, so 16 concurrent children\\\\n  sit far inside jupiter's 48 cores / 62 GB and shiva's 24 / 62;\\\\n- the lane's wall time is dominated by a few long namespaces\\\\n  (`yin.repl.python-antlr-test` has an explicit 3-hour step,\\\\n  `yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test` needs one — see\\\\n  `step-timeout-ms` in `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj`), so beyond ~16 workers\\\\n  the critical path is the longest child, not the count;\\\\n- **parallel is unproven until checked once**: the recorded lane evidence\\\\n  (J2 closure, the safepoint 60/265,225/0 pass) was all produced `jobs 1`.\\\\n  The first parallel run must be compared per-namespace against a serial\\\\n  baseline; any namespace that flips red gets one serial re-run to\\\\n  attribute before its evidence is trusted. Until that first comparison\\\\n  passes, keep `jobs 1` for anything whose verdict is timing-sensitive —\\\\n  timeout and wall-time measurements, native-memory/leak gates, and the\\\\n  merge-decision qualification runs.\\\\n\\\\nSuites that exceed the lane's default step budget are pinned in the lane's\\\\n`step-timeout-ms` map with their measured wall time; a namespace timing out\\\\nunder the default budget is a reason to measure it serially and add an\\\\nentry, not to re-run under the same bound.\\\\n\\\\n### The full run\\\\n\\\\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\\\\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\\\\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\\\\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\\\\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\\\\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (both hosts run all three lanes; a second clone for Dart is recommended)\\\\nset -u\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\"\\\\nDART=\\\\\\\"${2:-}\\\\\\\"\\\\ncd \\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\neval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\"\\\\nLOG=\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\"$LOG\\\\\\\"\\\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\\\necho \\\\\\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\\\\\\\" | tee \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\\\\\"$LOG/npm.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1               && echo \\\\\\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-node.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\\\\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\\\\\"$LOG/antlr.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1         && echo \\\\\\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-peer.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\\\\\"dart peer built $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \\\\\\\"$@\\\\\\\" >\\\\\\\"$LOG/$name.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\n         echo \\\\\\\"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"; }\\\\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\\\\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\\\\nif [ -n \\\\\\\"$DART\\\\\\\" ]; then                            # its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\\\\n  ( cd \\\\\\\"$DART\\\\\\\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1\\\\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\\\\nfi\\\\nwait\\\\necho \\\\\\\"done $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \\\\\\\"bash -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"'\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\\\\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\\\\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\\\\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\\\\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\\\\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\\\\n(measured 2026-10-11).\\\\n\\\\n### Known differences from the laptop\\\\n\\\\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\\\\n  The assertion \\\\\\\"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\\\\\\\" gets no `bind-failed`\\\\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\\\\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\\\\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\\\\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\\\\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\\\\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\\\\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\\\\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\\\\n\\\\n### The nightly full suite and the branch-testing rule (2026-10-30 owner ruling)\\\\n\\\\nOwner, verbatim: \\\\\\\"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\\\\ncompletely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\\\\nbranch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\\\\nchanges\\\\\\\" — and, clarifying the nightly's target: \\\\\\\"it should not be for\\\\nonly a worktree/branch. the nightly complete test should be for master.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nTwo tiers, by target and purpose:\\\\n\\\\n- **The nightly full suite runs MASTER ONLY** (`~/ci/nightly-full.sh` on\\\\n  shiva, tmux session `nightly`, scheduled 03:00 daily through the\\\\n  orchestrator's automation; it fetches and checks out origin/master\\\\n  before anything runs). Every lane, every host target: the JVM full\\\\n  suite, the full Node suite, the full Dart suite. Its results are a\\\\n  post-hoc integrity net for master, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\\\\n  finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\\\\n- **Branch/worktree testing runs only the namespaces that target the\\\\n  changed code** (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff\\\\n  touches), one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node)\\\\n  when cljc goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch:\\\\n  a branch gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree\\\\n  re-proven. A branch is never promoted by its own testing — the merge\\\\n  to master is what puts it under the nightly.\\\\n\\\\nAn orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\\\\nsign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\\\\nnightly owns the follow-up.\\\\n\\\\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\\\\n\\\\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\\\\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\\\\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\\\\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\\\\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\\\\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\\\\n\\\\n### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\\\\n\\\\nFirst full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\\\\nANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\\\\nreplaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\\\\n(`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\\\\n**Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\\\\nlaptop — route Node lanes to the laptop or shiva); **Dart quick tier\\\\nrc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\\\\n(`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\\\\ntaken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\\\\nis the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\\\\ntime matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\\\\nmedians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\\\\n\\\\nOne exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\\\\n`gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\\\\nsame time — they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\\\\n\\\\\\\"Patch does not apply\\\\\\\" or \\\\\\\"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\\\\\\\".\\\\nRun them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\\\\nThe fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\\\\ngenerator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\\\\none week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\\\\n\\\\n### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\\\\n\\\\nOwner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs — `claude`, `codex`, `glm` —\\\\nare installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\\\\norchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \\\\\\\"run tests remotely\\\\nover SSH\\\\\\\" to \\\\\\\"delegate whole work agents remotely\\\\\\\": a heavy implementation or\\\\nverification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\\\\nsuites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\\\\norchestrator's laptop unloaded.\\\\n\\\\nThe pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\\\\nreference):\\\\n\\\\n1. **Land the branch on origin first** (push from the laptop; never rsync) and\\\\n   prepare the scratch clone per \\\\\\\"Get the code there\\\\\\\".\\\\n2. **Start a named tmux session** for the task; run the agent inside it with\\\\n   the toolchain activated *inside* the tmux command (`eval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s\\\\n   bash)\\\\\\\"` after the `cd` — the non-interactive SSH shell does not have it).\\\\n   A fresh clone hits mise's interactive trust prompt, which blocks the whole\\\\n   session until answered via `tmux send-keys`; pre-empt it with `mise trust`\\\\n   inside the same command.\\\\n3. **The remote agent works in the clone**: implement, build, run lanes,\\\\n   write its findings artifact — the same brief form as any delegate dispatch\\\\n   (role template, bounded file ownership, deliverable path), just executing\\\\n   on the host. Long jobs survive SSH drops because of tmux.\\\\n4. **The orchestrator polls detached files, never the pane** (pane captures\\\\n   carry control characters that make output look empty): rc files, summary\\\\n   logs and `ls *.flag` sentinels written by the run.\\\\n5. **Division of labor**: bookkeeping and polling are orchestrator SSH\\\\n   one-liners; execution belongs to the remote agent. Do not run heavy lanes\\\\n   from the orchestrator's laptop while a remote agent owns the same gate.\\\\n6. **Same trust rules as any delegate**: remote execution does not waive\\\\n   Workflow steps 6–7 of the orchestrator role — artifacts are verified, and\\\\n   review before commit applies to remote-produced work identically.\\\\n   Session-ID capture and resume rules from the delegate-invocation reference\\\\n   apply per host (each host's `~/.claude`, `~/.codex` store is its own).\\\\n\\\\nKnown state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\\\\njolt canonical-fixes verification ran there); `glm` reported available by the\\\\nowner on both hosts, not yet exercised. Both hosts are toolchain-verified for\\\\nthe full tri-host suite (jupiter verified 2026-10-29 — see Measured times;\\\\nits 3.41.9 Flutter was replaced with 3.47.8, and model availability per\\\\nhost is governed by `routing-status.md`, not this file).\\\\n\\\\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\\\\n\\\\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\\\\n\\\\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\\\\n\\\\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\\\\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\\\\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\\\\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\\\\n\\\\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\\\\n\\\\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\\\\n\\\\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\\\\n\\\\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\\\\n   ```sh\\\\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\\\\n   ```\\\\n\\\\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\\\\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\\\\n\\\\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\\\\n   Common patterns:\\\\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\\\\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\\\\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\\\\n\\\\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\\\\n\\\\n**The merge precondition (2026-10-30 owner ruling, restored 2026-10-31 —\\\\na dedup pass dropped this section along with a stale duplicate of the\\\\ntwo-tier policy above; both stand):** before any merge into master, the\\\\n`dao.stream` tests must run and pass — dao.stream is what everything is\\\\nbuilt on. Concretely: `bb test:sub dao.stream` (the subsystem's tests,\\\\n`--slow` when the slice touches slow-guarded surfaces) is part of every\\\\nbranch gate regardless of which subsystems the diff's own changes\\\\nselect, because a change can break the foundation without touching it.\\\\nA red dao.stream blocks the merge until fixed or diagnosed as\\\\npre-existing by the owning seat.\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ncollab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.prompt.md:58:   \\\"unpinned\\\". Host-to-host SSH verified working both directions.\\ncollab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.prompt.md:87:    unpinned-fallback sound? What failure modes does the fallback hide, and\\ncollab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.prompt.md:88:    should an unpinned run be treated as advisory?\\ncollab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:11:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_4\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md && cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md'\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"---\\\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# BUILD & TEST\\\\n\\\\n## Commands\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\\\\\"Remote test servers\\\\\\\" below for\\\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\\\n#\\\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\\\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\\\\\"Remote test servers\\\\\\\" below).\\\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\\\n\\\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\\\n\\\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\\\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\\\\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\\\\n\\\\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\\\\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\\\\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\\\\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\\\\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\\\\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\\\\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\\\\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\\\\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\\\\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\\\\n\\\\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\\\\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\\\\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\\\\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\\\\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\\\\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\\\\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\\\\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\\\\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\\\\n\\\\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\\\\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\\\\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\\\\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\\\\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\\\\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\\\\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\\\\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\\\\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\\\\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\\\\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\\\\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\\\\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\\\\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\\\\n\\\\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\\\\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\\\\n\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\\\\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\\\\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\\\\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\\\\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\\\\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\\\\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n\\\\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\\\\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\\\\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\\\\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\\\\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\\\\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\\\\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\\\\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\\\\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\\\\n\\\\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\\\\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\\\\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\\\\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\\\\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\\\\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\\\\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\\\\n\\\\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\\\\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\\\\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\\\\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\\\\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\\\\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\\\\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\\\\navailable. Do not run another lane set concurrently on the laptop.\\\\n\\\\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\\\\n\\\\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\\\\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\\\\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\\\\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\\\\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\\\\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\\\\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\\\\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\\\\n\\\\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\\\\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\\\\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\\\\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\\\\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\\\\n\\\\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\\\\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\\\\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\\\\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\\\\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\\\\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\\\\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\\\\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\\\\n\\\\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\\\\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\\\\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\\\\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\\\\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\\\\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\\\\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\\\\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\\\\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\\\\n\\\\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\\\\n\\\\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\\\\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\\\\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\\\\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\\\\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\\\\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\\\\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\\\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\\\\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\\\\n\\\\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\\\\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\\\\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\\\\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\\\\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\\\\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\\\\n\\\\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\\\\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\\\\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\\\\n\\\\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\\\\n\\\\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\\\\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\\\\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\\\\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\\\\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \\\\\\\"Test tiers: quick and slow\\\\\\\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\\\\nslice L5) is two tests:\\\\n\\\\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\\\\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\\\\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\\\\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\\\\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\\\\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\\\\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\\\\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\\\\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\\\\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\\\\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\\\\n  REPL.\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\\\\n\\\\n# ClojureDart\\\\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\\\n\\\\n# Linting\\\\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\\\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\\\\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\\\\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\\\\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\\\\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\\\\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\\\\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\\\\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\\\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\\\\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\\\\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\\\\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\\\\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\\\\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\\\\n\\\\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\\\\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\\\\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\\\\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\\\\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\\\\n\\\\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\\\\n\\\\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\\\\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\\\\n\\\\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\\\\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\\\\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\\\\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\\\\\\\"When to tag a test slow\\\\\\\"): a deftest that\\\\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\\\\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\\\\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\\\\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\\\\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\\\\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\\\\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\\\\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\\\\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\\\\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\\\\n\\\\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \\\\\\\"Remote test\\\\nservers\\\\\\\" for how to run them):\\\\n\\\\n| Run | Time |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\\\\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\\\\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\\\\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\\\\n\\\\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\\\\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\\\\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\\\\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\\\\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\\\\n\\\\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\\\\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\\\\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\\\\\\\"test\\\\\\\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\\\\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\\\\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\\\\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\\\\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\\\\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\\\\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\\\\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\\\\nset -u\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\"\\\\ncd \\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\nPAR=\\\\\\\"${2:-8}\\\\\\\"\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\neval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\"\\\\nOUT=\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci/shards\\\\\\\"; rm -rf \\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\"\\\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\\\necho \\\\\\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\\\\\" | tee \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\\\\\"$OUT/npm.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1                   && echo \\\\\\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\\\\\"$OUT/build-node.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1     && echo \\\\\\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\\\\\"$OUT/antlr.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1             && echo \\\\\\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nSUBS=\\\\\\\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\\\\\\\"\\\\n: > \\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nfor s in $SUBS; do\\\\n  bb test:sub:list \\\\\\\"$s\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null | awk \\\\\\\"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\");print}\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n  cat \\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\"$s $(wc -l < \\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\"$OUT/plan.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\ndone\\\\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\\\\nfind test -type f \\\\\\\\( -name \\\\\\\"*_test.clj\\\\\\\" -o -name \\\\\\\"*_test.cljc\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\) | sed -e \\\\\\\"s#^test/##\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\"s#\\\\\\\\.cljc\\\\\\\\?\\\\\\\\$##\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\"s#/#.#g\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\"s#_#-#g\\\\\\\" | sort -u > \\\\\\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nsort -u \\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\\\\\"\\\\ncomm -23 \\\\\\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\necho \\\\\\\"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nrun_shard() {\\\\n  s=$1; f=\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\"; [ -s \\\\\\\"$f\\\\\\\" ] || return 0\\\\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \\\\\\\"$n\\\\\\\"); done < \\\\\\\"$f\\\\\\\"\\\\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \\\\\\\"${args[@]}\\\\\\\" >\\\\\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \\\\\\\"^Ran \\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\\\\\" | tail -1)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n}\\\\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\\\\nprintf \\\\\\\"%s\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \\\\\\\"$PAR\\\\\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\\\\\"run_shard {}\\\\\\\"\\\\necho \\\\\\\"done $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\\\\nset -u\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\"\\\\ncd \\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\nPAR=\\\\\\\"${2:-6}\\\\\\\"\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\neval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\"\\\\nOUT=\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\\\\\\\"; rm -rf \\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\"\\\\nrun_ns() {\\\\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\\\\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \\\\\\\"$n\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \\\\\\\"^Ran \\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\\\\\" | tail -1)\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n}\\\\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\\\\necho \\\\\\\"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nxargs -P \\\\\\\"$PAR\\\\\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\\\\\"run_ns {}\\\\\\\" < \\\\\\\"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\necho \\\\\\\"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\\\\n\\\\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\\\\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\\\\nand `git`.\\\\n\\\\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\\\\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot (rechecked 2026-10-29): java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.8 (the stale hand-symlinked 3.41.9 was replaced via `mise install flutter@3.47.8` after it failed to compile `dart:ui`-using cljd namespaces; if the Dart lane dies in the cljd compile, check `mise ls flutter` first). Tri-host quick tier verified green on 2026-10-29 (see Measured times). |\\\\n\\\\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\\\\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\\\\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\\\\n\\\\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\\\\n\\\\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\\\\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\\\\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\\\\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\\\\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\\\\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\\\\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\\\\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\\\\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \\\\\\\"$URL\\\\\\\";\\\\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\\\\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\\\\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\\\\n\\\\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\\\\n\\\\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\\\\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\\\\ninto the clone:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\\\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\\\\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\\\\neval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\\\\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\\\\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \\\\\\\"Can't\\\\nfind flutter on PATH\\\\\\\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\\\\n\\\\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\\\\n\\\\n\\\\\\\"One lane set at a time\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\"one Dart lane at a time\\\\\\\" are per-machine rules about\\\\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\\\\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\\\\\\\"on one machine where\\\\navoidable\\\\\\\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\\\\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\\\\n\\\\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\\\\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\\\\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\\\\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\\\\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\\\\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\\\\n  by the design's own reasoning (\\\\\\\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\\\\\\\"), not yet\\\\n  verified by a measured run.\\\\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\\\\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\\\\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\\\\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\\\\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\\\\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\\\\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\\\\n\\\\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\\\\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\\\\nseparate Dart clone.\\\\n\\\\n### The jolt J2 lane: run it with `--jobs` on the servers\\\\n\\\\nThe jolt lane's core mode (`bb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core [--jobs N]\\\\n[--only re] [--timeout-ms M]`) runs each test namespace as its own child jolt\\\\nprocess (own Chez VM, own native parser library load, own log under\\\\n`build/j2-logs/core/`), so the namespaces cannot corrupt one another — the\\\\nonly thing `--jobs N` overlaps is the waiting. The default is `jobs 1`\\\\n(strictly serial), which on jupiter makes the full ~179-namespace lane a\\\\n5-6-hour run.\\\\n\\\\nOn the servers, run it with `--jobs 16`:\\\\n\\\\n- jolt children are essentially single-threaded (the 16 MiB parse thread\\\\n  only burns during parses) and cost 1-2 GB each, so 16 concurrent children\\\\n  sit far inside jupiter's 48 cores / 62 GB and shiva's 24 / 62;\\\\n- the lane's wall time is dominated by a few long namespaces\\\\n  (`yin.repl.python-antlr-test` has an explicit 3-hour step,\\\\n  `yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test` needs one — see\\\\n  `step-timeout-ms` in `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj`), so beyond ~16 workers\\\\n  the critical path is the longest child, not the count;\\\\n- **parallel is unproven until checked once**: the recorded lane evidence\\\\n  (J2 closure, the safepoint 60/265,225/0 pass) was all produced `jobs 1`.\\\\n  The first parallel run must be compared per-namespace against a serial\\\\n  baseline; any namespace that flips red gets one serial re-run to\\\\n  attribute before its evidence is trusted. Until that first comparison\\\\n  passes, keep `jobs 1` for anything whose verdict is timing-sensitive —\\\\n  timeout and wall-time measurements, native-memory/leak gates, and the\\\\n  merge-decision qualification runs.\\\\n\\\\nSuites that exceed the lane's default step budget are pinned in the lane's\\\\n`step-timeout-ms` map with their measured wall time; a namespace timing out\\\\nunder the default budget is a reason to measure it serially and add an\\\\nentry, not to re-run under the same bound.\\\\n\\\\n### The full run\\\\n\\\\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\\\\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\\\\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\\\\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\\\\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\\\\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (both hosts run all three lanes; a second clone for Dart is recommended)\\\\nset -u\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\"\\\\nDART=\\\\\\\"${2:-}\\\\\\\"\\\\ncd \\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\neval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\"\\\\nLOG=\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\"$LOG\\\\\\\"\\\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\\\necho \\\\\\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\\\\\\\" | tee \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\\\\\"$LOG/npm.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1               && echo \\\\\\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-node.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\\\\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\\\\\"$LOG/antlr.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1         && echo \\\\\\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-peer.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\\\\\"dart peer built $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \\\\\\\"$@\\\\\\\" >\\\\\\\"$LOG/$name.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\n         echo \\\\\\\"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"; }\\\\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\\\\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\\\\nif [ -n \\\\\\\"$DART\\\\\\\" ]; then                            # its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\\\\n  ( cd \\\\\\\"$DART\\\\\\\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1\\\\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\\\\nfi\\\\nwait\\\\necho \\\\\\\"done $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \\\\\\\"bash -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"'\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\\\\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\\\\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\\\\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\\\\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\\\\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\\\\n(measured 2026-10-11).\\\\n\\\\n### Known differences from the laptop\\\\n\\\\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\\\\n  The assertion \\\\\\\"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\\\\\\\" gets no `bind-failed`\\\\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\\\\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\\\\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\\\\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\\\\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\\\\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\\\\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\\\\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\\\\n\\\\n### The nightly full suite and the branch-testing rule (2026-10-30 owner ruling)\\\\n\\\\nOwner, verbatim: \\\\\\\"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\\\\ncompletely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\\\\nbranch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\\\\nchanges\\\\\\\" — and, clarifying the nightly's target: \\\\\\\"it should not be for\\\\nonly a worktree/branch. the nightly complete test should be for master.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nTwo tiers, by target and purpose:\\\\n\\\\n- **The nightly full suite runs MASTER ONLY** (`~/ci/nightly-full.sh` on\\\\n  shiva, tmux session `nightly`, scheduled 03:00 daily through the\\\\n  orchestrator's automation; it fetches and checks out origin/master\\\\n  before anything runs). Every lane, every host target: the JVM full\\\\n  suite, the full Node suite, the full Dart suite. Its results are a\\\\n  post-hoc integrity net for master, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\\\\n  finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\\\\n- **Branch/worktree testing runs only the namespaces that target the\\\\n  changed code** (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff\\\\n  touches), one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node)\\\\n  when cljc goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch:\\\\n  a branch gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree\\\\n  re-proven. A branch is never promoted by its own testing — the merge\\\\n  to master is what puts it under the nightly.\\\\n\\\\nAn orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\\\\nsign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\\\\nnightly owns the follow-up.\\\\n\\\\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\\\\n\\\\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\\\\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\\\\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\\\\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\\\\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\\\\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\\\\n\\\\n### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\\\\n\\\\nFirst full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\\\\nANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\\\\nreplaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\\\\n(`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\\\\n**Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\\\\nlaptop — route Node lanes to the laptop or shiva); **Dart quick tier\\\\nrc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\\\\n(`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\\\\ntaken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\\\\nis the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\\\\ntime matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\\\\nmedians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\\\\n\\\\nOne exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\\\\n`gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\\\\nsame time — they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\\\\n\\\\\\\"Patch does not apply\\\\\\\" or \\\\\\\"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\\\\\\\".\\\\nRun them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\\\\nThe fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\\\\ngenerator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\\\\none week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\\\\n\\\\n### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\\\\n\\\\nOwner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs — `claude`, `codex`, `glm` —\\\\nare installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\\\\norchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \\\\\\\"run tests remotely\\\\nover SSH\\\\\\\" to \\\\\\\"delegate whole work agents remotely\\\\\\\": a heavy implementation or\\\\nverification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\\\\nsuites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\\\\norchestrator's laptop unloaded.\\\\n\\\\nThe pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\\\\nreference):\\\\n\\\\n1. **Land the branch on origin first** (push from the laptop; never rsync) and\\\\n   prepare the scratch clone per \\\\\\\"Get the code there\\\\\\\".\\\\n2. **Start a named tmux session** for the task; run the agent inside it with\\\\n   the toolchain activated *inside* the tmux command (`eval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s\\\\n   bash)\\\\\\\"` after the `cd` — the non-interactive SSH shell does not have it).\\\\n   A fresh clone hits mise's interactive trust prompt, which blocks the whole\\\\n   session until answered via `tmux send-keys`; pre-empt it with `mise trust`\\\\n   inside the same command.\\\\n3. **The remote agent works in the clone**: implement, build, run lanes,\\\\n   write its findings artifact — the same brief form as any delegate dispatch\\\\n   (role template, bounded file ownership, deliverable path), just executing\\\\n   on the host. Long jobs survive SSH drops because of tmux.\\\\n4. **The orchestrator polls detached files, never the pane** (pane captures\\\\n   carry control characters that make output look empty): rc files, summary\\\\n   logs and `ls *.flag` sentinels written by the run.\\\\n5. **Division of labor**: bookkeeping and polling are orchestrator SSH\\\\n   one-liners; execution belongs to the remote agent. Do not run heavy lanes\\\\n   from the orchestrator's laptop while a remote agent owns the same gate.\\\\n6. **Same trust rules as any delegate**: remote execution does not waive\\\\n   Workflow steps 6–7 of the orchestrator role — artifacts are verified, and\\\\n   review before commit applies to remote-produced work identically.\\\\n   Session-ID capture and resume rules from the delegate-invocation reference\\\\n   apply per host (each host's `~/.claude`, `~/.codex` store is its own).\\\\n\\\\nKnown state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\\\\njolt canonical-fixes verification ran there); `glm` reported available by the\\\\nowner on both hosts, not yet exercised. Both hosts are toolchain-verified for\\\\nthe full tri-host suite (jupiter verified 2026-10-29 — see Measured times;\\\\nits 3.41.9 Flutter was replaced with 3.47.8, and model availability per\\\\nhost is governed by `routing-status.md`, not this file).\\\\n\\\\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\\\\n\\\\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\\\\n\\\\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\\\\n\\\\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\\\\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\\\\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\\\\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\\\\n\\\\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\\\\n\\\\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\\\\n\\\\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\\\\n\\\\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\\\\n   ```sh\\\\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\\\\n   ```\\\\n\\\\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\\\\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\\\\n\\\\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\\\\n   Common patterns:\\\\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\\\\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\\\\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\\\\n\\\\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\\\\n\\\\n**The merge precondition (2026-10-30 owner ruling, restored 2026-10-31 —\\\\na dedup pass dropped this section along with a stale duplicate of the\\\\ntwo-tier policy above; both stand):** before any merge into master, the\\\\n`dao.stream` tests must run and pass — dao.stream is what everything is\\\\nbuilt on. Concretely: `bb test:sub dao.stream` (the subsystem's tests,\\\\n`--slow` when the slice touches slow-guarded surfaces) is part of every\\\\nbranch gate regardless of which subsystems the diff's own changes\\\\nselect, because a change can break the foundation without touching it.\\\\nA red dao.stream blocks the merge until fixed or diagnosed as\\\\npre-existing by the owning seat.\\\\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\\\\n\\\\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\\\\n\\\\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\\\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\\\\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\\\\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\\\\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\\\\n\\\\n### A. Logic & Correctness\\\\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\\\\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\\\\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\\\\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\\\\n\\\\n### B. Security & Credentials\\\\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\\\\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\\\\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\\\\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\\\\n\\\\n### C. Architecture & API Design\\\\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\\\\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\\\\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\\\\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\\\\n\\\\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\\\\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\\\\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\\\\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\\\\n\\\\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\\\\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\\\\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\\\\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\\\\n\\\\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\\\\n2. **Explain the \\\\\\\"Why\\\\\\\"**: Don't just say \\\\\\\"change this\\\\\\\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \\\\\\\"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\\\\\\\").\\\\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\\\\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ncollab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:13:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_6\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n --hidden -g '\\\\\\\"'!.git/**'\\\\\\\"' 'nightly-pin|node-shard|dart-shard|shard-jvm|nightly-results|unpinned' .\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\ncollab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:14:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_6\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n --hidden -g '\\\\\\\"'!.git/**'\\\\\\\"' 'nightly-pin|node-shard|dart-shard|shard-jvm|nightly-results|unpinned' .\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"./collab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:5:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pwd && rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g '*shard*' -g '*nightly*' -g 'cljd_agg.clj' -g 'subsystems.edn' -g 'bb.edn' -g 'datom.world.md'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld\\\\\\\\ndocs/design/datom.world.md\\\\\\\\nbb.edn\\\\\\\\nAGENTS.md\\\\\\\\ncollab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\\\\\\\\nsrc/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\\\\\\\nsrc/dev/quick_sharded.clj\\\\\\\\nsrc/dev/subsystems.edn\\\\\\\\ncollab/yin-ucf/logs/d15-node-shard-build.log\\\\\\\\nsrc/dev/quick_sharded_test.clj\\\\\\\\ncollab/1793480000000-reviewer-nightly-red-gate.prompt.md\\\\\\\\ncollab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.prompt.md\\\\\\\\ncollab/1793465000000-engineer-nightly-red-fix.codex.stdout.log\\\\\\\\ncollab/1793480000000-reviewer-nightly-red-gate.kimi.stdout.log\\\\\\\\ncollab/1793465000000-engineer-nightly-red-fix.prompt.md\\\\\\\\ncollab/1793456746000-orchestrator-yangpython-nightly-red.prompt.md\\\\\\\\ncollab/1793465000000-engineer-nightly-red-fix.muse.stdout.log\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n./src/dev/cljd_agg.clj:147:   --shard-dir overrides, which lets dart-shard.sh run one directory per\\\\n./src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj:204:          (and helper? (nil? pin)) (note! :helper-token-unpinned [(:line ke) (:column ke)])\\\\n./collab/yin-ucf/1791312000000-reviewer-d13-candidate-gate-r3.opus.stdout.log:69:**r2-2 (the judge refusal leading to a fresh proposal was unpinned).**\\\\n./collab/yin-ucf/1791312000000-reviewer-d13-candidate-gate-r3.opus.stdout.log:73:- That run goes through `step-proposing`'s no-grant path, then `propose`, then `refused?`, then a mint, which is exactly the branch that was unpinned.\\\\n./collab/yin-ucf/1791195500000-reviewer-d2-renewal-gate.glm.findings.md:12:**1. (Blocker) The judge-counting assertion is vacuous — the contract's fourth behavior is unpinned.**\\\\n./collab/1793480000000-reviewer-nightly-red-gate.kimi.stdout.log:5571: .../yin-ucf/logs/d15-node-shard-build.log          |   0\\\\n./collab/1793480000000-reviewer-nightly-red-gate.kimi.stdout.log:29677:diff --git a/collab/yin-ucf/logs/d15-node-shard-build.log b/archive/yin-ucf/logs/d15-node-shard-build.log\\\\n./collab/1793480000000-reviewer-nightly-red-gate.kimi.stdout.log:29679:rename from collab/yin-ucf/logs/d15-node-shard-build.log\\\\n./collab/1793480000000-reviewer-nightly-red-gate.kimi.stdout.log:29680:rename to archive/yin-ucf/logs/d15-node-shard-build.log\\\\n./collab/1793480000000-reviewer-nightly-red-gate.kimi.stdout.log:37746:-  cron log: ~/ci/nightly-cron.log; per-stamp summaries: ~/ci/nightly-results/. Cost of the\\\\n./collab/1793480000000-reviewer-nightly-red-gate.kimi.stdout.log:37753:-  ~/ci/shard-jvm.sh (subsystem shards + an uncovered catch-all, verified coverage 276/316 + 40\\\\n./collab/1793480000000-reviewer-nightly-red-gate.kimi.stdout.log:37756:-  owner's shiva-vs-jupiter comparison (the jupiter copy needed shard-jvm.sh copied over — my\\\\n./collab/orchestration/1792610000000-orchestrator-handoff-phase8.md:15:2. Phase 8: create `srvm-phase8` off master (push), slice branches `srvm-phase8ab` (descriptors + BLAKE3 together) and `srvm-phase8d` (old transport out) from it, codex briefs, Fable/opus review, Node/Dart/quick tier per slice on shiva/jupiter (scripts `~/ci/shard-jvm.sh`, `~/ci/hosts*.sh` pattern, `~/ci/slow-tier.sh`, `~/ci/stage-e.sh`; Dart without DATOM_SLOW_TESTS unless a slow-tagged ns is the target; one Dart lane per host; JVM and Dart not in the same clone at once).\\\\n./archive/yang-python/810-common-call/1793430000000-engineer-s3-linked-fix.codex.stdout.log:7:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md && rg -n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"yang.call|linked-source|linked source|closure|totals|deferred|gate_test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" src test scripts deps.edn'\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"---\\\\\\\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# BUILD & TEST\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Commands\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\\\\\\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\\\\\\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\\\\\\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\\\\\\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\\\\\\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\\\\\\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\\\\\\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\\\\\\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\\\\\\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\\\\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\\\\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\\\\\\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\\\\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\\\\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\\\\\\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\\\\\\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\\\\\\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\\\\\\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\\\\\\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\\\\\\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\\\\\\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\\\\\\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\\\\\\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Remote test servers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" below for\\\\\\\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\\\\\\\n#\\\\\\\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\\\\\\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\\\\\\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\\\\\\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\\\\\\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\\\\\\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\\\\\\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\\\\\\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\\\\\\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\\\\\\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\\\\\\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\\\\\\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\\\\\\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\\\\\\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\\\\\\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\\\\\\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\\\\\\\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\\\\\\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\\\\\\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\\\\\\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Remote test servers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" below).\\\\\\\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\\\\\\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\\\\\\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\\\\\\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\\\\\\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\\\\\\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\\\\\\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\\\\\\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\\\\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\\\\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\\\\\\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\\\\\\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\\\\\\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\\\\\\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\\\\\\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\\\\\\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\\\\\\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\\\\\\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\\\\\\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\\\\\\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\\\\\\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\\\\\\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\\\\\\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\\\\\\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\\\\\\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\\\\\\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\\\\\\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\\\\\\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\\\\\\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\\\\\\\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\\\\\\\\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\\\\\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\\\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\\\\\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\\\\\\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\\\\\\\\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\\\\\\\\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\\\\\\\\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\\\\\\\\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\\\\\\\\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\\\\\\\\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\\\\\\\\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\\\\\\\\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\\\\\\\\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\\\\\\\\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\\\\\\\\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\\\\\\\\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\\\\\\\\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\\\\\\\\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\\\\\\\\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\\\\\\\\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\\\\\\\\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\\\\\\\\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\\\\\\\\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\\\\\\\\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\\\\\\\\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\\\\\\\\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\\\\\\\\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\\\\\\\\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\\\\\\\\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\\\\\\\\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\\\\\\\\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\\\\\\\\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\\\\\\\\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\\\\\\\\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\\\\\\\\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\\\\\\\\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\\\\\\\\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\\\\\\\\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\\\\\\\\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\\\\\\\\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\\\\\\\\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\\\\\\\\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\\\\\\\\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\\\\\\\\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\\\\\\\\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\\\\\\\\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\\\\\\\\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\\\\\\\\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\\\\\\\\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\\\\\\\\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\\\\\\\\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\\\\\\\\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\\\\\\\\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\\\\\\\\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\\\\\\\\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\\\\\\\\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\\\\\\\\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\\\\\\\\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\\\\\\\\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\\\\\\\\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\\\\\\\\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\\\\\\\\navailable. Do not run another lane set concurrently on the laptop.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\\\\\\\\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\\\\\\\\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\\\\\\\\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\\\\\\\\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\\\\\\\\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\\\\\\\\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\\\\\\\\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\\\\\\\\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\\\\\\\\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\\\\\\\\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\\\\\\\\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\\\\\\\\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\\\\\\\\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\\\\\\\\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\\\\\\\\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\\\\\\\\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\\\\\\\\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\\\\\\\\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\\\\\\\\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\\\\\\\\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\\\\\\\\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\\\\\\\\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\\\\\\\\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\\\\\\\\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\\\\\\\\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\\\\\\\\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\\\\\\\\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\\\\\\\\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\\\\\\\\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\\\\\\\\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\\\\\\\\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\\\\\\\\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\\\\\\\\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\\\\\\\\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\\\\\\\\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\\\\\\\\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\\\\\\\\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\\\\\\\\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\\\\\\\\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\\\\\\\\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Test tiers: quick and slow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\\\\\\\\nslice L5) is two tests:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\\\\\\\\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\\\\\\\\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\\\\\\\\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\\\\\\\\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\\\\\\\\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\\\\\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\\\\\\\\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\\\\\\\\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\\\\\\\\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\\\\\\\\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\\\\\\\\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\\\\\\\\n  REPL.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\\\\\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\\\\\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\\\\\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# ClojureDart\\\\\\\\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# Linting\\\\\\\\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\\\\\\\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\\\\\\\\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\\\\\\\\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\\\\\\\\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\\\\\\\\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\\\\\\\\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\\\\\\\\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\\\\\\\\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\\\\\\\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\\\\\\\\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\\\\\\\\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\\\\\\\\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\\\\\\\\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\\\\\\\\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\\\\\\\\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\\\\\\\\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\\\\\\\\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\\\\\\\\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\\\\\\\\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\\\\\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\\\\\\\\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\\\\\\\\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\\\\\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\\\\\\\\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"When to tag a test slow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"): a deftest that\\\\\\\\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\\\\\\\\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\\\\\\\\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\\\\\\\\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\\\\\\\\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\\\\\\\\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\\\\\\\\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\\\\\\\\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\\\\\\\\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\\\\\\\\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Remote test\\\\\\\\nservers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" for how to run them):\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Run | Time |\\\\\\\\n|---|---|\\\\\\\\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\\\\\\\\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\\\\\\\\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\\\\\\\\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\\\\\\\\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\\\\\\\\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\\\\\\\\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\\\\\\\\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\\\\\\\\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\\\\\\\\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\\\\\\\\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\\\\\\\\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\\\\\\\\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\\\\\\\\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\\\\\\\\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\\\\\\\\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\\\\\\\\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\\\\\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\\\\\\\\nset -u\\\\\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\ncd \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\\\\\nPAR=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${2:-8}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\\\\\neval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nOUT=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci/shards\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; rm -rf \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/npm.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1                   && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/build-node.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1     && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/antlr.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1             && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nSUBS=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n: > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nfor s in $SUBS; do\\\\\\\\n  bb test:sub:list \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$s\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null | awk \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\");print}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n  cat \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$s $(wc -l < \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/plan.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\ndone\\\\\\\\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\\\\\\\\nfind test -type f \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\( -name \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"*_test.clj\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -o -name \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"*_test.cljc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\) | sed -e \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"s#^test/##\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"s#\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.cljc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\$##\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"s#/#.#g\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"s#_#-#g\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | sort -u > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nsort -u \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\ncomm -23 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nrun_shard() {\\\\\\\\n  s=$1; f=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; [ -s \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$f\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" ] || return 0\\\\\\\\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"); done < \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$f\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${args[@]}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^Ran \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tail -1)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\\\\\\\\nprintf \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"%s\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$PAR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"run_shard {}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"done $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\\\\\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\\\\\\\\nset -u\\\\\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\ncd \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\\\\\nPAR=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${2:-6}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\\\\\neval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nOUT=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; rm -rf \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nrun_ns() {\\\\\\\\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\\\\\\\\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^Ran \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tail -1)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nxargs -P \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$PAR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"run_ns {}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" < \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\\\\\\\\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\\\\\\\\nand `git`.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\\\\\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\\\\\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\\\\\\\\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot (rechecked 2026-10-29): java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.8 (the stale hand-symlinked 3.41.9 was replaced via `mise install flutter@3.47.8` after it failed to compile `dart:ui`-using cljd namespaces; if the Dart lane dies in the cljd compile, check `mise ls flutter` first). Tri-host quick tier verified green on 2026-10-29 (see Measured times). |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\\\\\\\\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\\\\\\\\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\\\\\\\\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\\\\\\\\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\\\\\\\\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\\\\\\\\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\\\\\\\\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\\\\\\\\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\\\\\\\\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\\\\\\\\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$URL\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\";\\\\\\\\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\\\\\\\\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\\\\\\\\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\\\\\\\\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\\\\\\\\ninto the clone:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\\\\\\\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\\\\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\\\\\\\\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\\\\\\\\neval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\\\\\\\\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\\\\\\\\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Can't\\\\\\\\nfind flutter on PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"One lane set at a time\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"one Dart lane at a time\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" are per-machine rules about\\\\\\\\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\\\\\\\\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"on one machine where\\\\\\\\navoidable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\\\\\\\\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\\\\\\\\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\\\\\\\\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\\\\\\\\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\\\\\\\\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\\\\\\\\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\\\\\\\\n  by the design's own reasoning (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"), not yet\\\\\\\\n  verified by a measured run.\\\\\\\\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\\\\\\\\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\\\\\\\\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\\\\\\\\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\\\\\\\\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\\\\\\\\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\\\\\\\\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\\\\\\\\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\\\\\\\\nseparate Dart clone.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### The jolt J2 lane: run it with `--jobs` on the servers\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe jolt lane's core mode (`bb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core [--jobs N]\\\\\\\\n[--only re] [--timeout-ms M]`) runs each test namespace as its own child jolt\\\\\\\\nprocess (own Chez VM, own native parser library load, own log under\\\\\\\\n`build/j2-logs/core/`), so the namespaces cannot corrupt one another — the\\\\\\\\nonly thing `--jobs N` overlaps is the waiting. The default is `jobs 1`\\\\\\\\n(strictly serial), which on jupiter makes the full ~179-namespace lane a\\\\\\\\n5-6-hour run.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOn the servers, run it with `--jobs 16`:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- jolt children are essentially single-threaded (the 16 MiB parse thread\\\\\\\\n  only burns during parses) and cost 1-2 GB each, so 16 concurrent children\\\\\\\\n  sit far inside jupiter's 48 cores / 62 GB and shiva's 24 / 62;\\\\\\\\n- the lane's wall time is dominated by a few long namespaces\\\\\\\\n  (`yin.repl.python-antlr-test` has an explicit 3-hour step,\\\\\\\\n  `yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test` needs one — see\\\\\\\\n  `step-timeout-ms` in `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj`), so beyond ~16 workers\\\\\\\\n  the critical path is the longest child, not the count;\\\\\\\\n- **parallel is unproven until checked once**: the recorded lane evidence\\\\\\\\n  (J2 closure, the safepoint 60/265,225/0 pass) was all produced `jobs 1`.\\\\\\\\n  The first parallel run must be compared per-namespace against a serial\\\\\\\\n  baseline; any namespace that flips red gets one serial re-run to\\\\\\\\n  attribute before its evidence is trusted. Until that first comparison\\\\\\\\n  passes, keep `jobs 1` for anything whose verdict is timing-sensitive —\\\\\\\\n  timeout and wall-time measurements, native-memory/leak gates, and the\\\\\\\\n  merge-decision qualification runs.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSuites that exceed the lane's default step budget are pinned in the lane's\\\\\\\\n`step-timeout-ms` map with their measured wall time; a namespace timing out\\\\\\\\nunder the default budget is a reason to measure it serially and add an\\\\\\\\nentry, not to re-run under the same bound.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### The full run\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\\\\\\\\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\\\\\\\\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\\\\\\\\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\\\\\\\\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\\\\\\\\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\\\\\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (both hosts run all three lanes; a second clone for Dart is recommended)\\\\\\\\nset -u\\\\\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nDART=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${2:-}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\ncd \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\\\\\neval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nLOG=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/npm.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1               && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-node.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/antlr.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1         && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-peer.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"dart peer built $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$@\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/$name.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\\\\\n         echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; }\\\\\\\\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\\\\\\\\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\\\\\\\\nif [ -n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$DART\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" ]; then                            # its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\\\\\\\\n  ( cd \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$DART\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1\\\\\\\\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\\\\\\\\nfi\\\\\\\\nwait\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"done $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"bash -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"'\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\\\\\\\\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\\\\\\\\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\\\\\\\\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\\\\\\\\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\\\\\\\\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\\\\\\\\n(measured 2026-10-11).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Known differences from the laptop\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\\\\\\\\n  The assertion \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" gets no `bind-failed`\\\\\\\\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\\\\\\\\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\\\\\\\\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\\\\\\\\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\\\\\\\\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\\\\\\\\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\\\\\\\\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\\\\\\\\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### The nightly full suite and the branch-testing rule (2026-10-30 owner ruling)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOwner, verbatim: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\\\\\\\\ncompletely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\\\\\\\\nbranch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\\\\\\\\nchanges.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTwo tiers, by purpose:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **The nightly full suite** (`~/ci/nightly-full.sh` on shiva, tmux\\\\\\\\n  session `nightly`, scheduled 03:00 daily through the orchestrator's\\\\\\\\n  automation): every lane, every host target, against origin/master —\\\\\\\\n  the JVM full suite, the full Node suite, the full Dart suite. Its\\\\\\\\n  results are a post-hoc integrity net, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\\\\\\\\n  finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\\\\\\\\n- **Branch/worktree testing**: only the namespaces that target the\\\\\\\\n  changed code (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff touches),\\\\\\\\n  one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node) when cljc\\\\\\\\n  goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch: a branch\\\\\\\\n  gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree re-proven.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAn orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\\\\\\\\nsign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\\\\\\\\nnightly owns the follow-up.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**The merge precondition (2026-10-30 owner ruling):** before any merge\\\\\\\\ninto master, the `dao.stream` tests must run and pass — dao.stream is\\\\\\\\nwhat everything is built on. Concretely: `bb test:sub dao.stream` (the\\\\\\\\nsubsystem's tests, `--slow` when the slice touches slow-guarded\\\\\\\\nsurfaces) is part of every branch gate regardless of which subsystems\\\\\\\\nthe diff's own changes select, because a change can break the foundation\\\\\\\\nwithout touching it. A red dao.stream blocks the merge until fixed or\\\\\\\\ndiagnosed as pre-existing by the owning seat.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### The nightly full suite and the branch-testing rule (2026-10-30 owner ruling)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOwner, verbatim: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\\\\\\\\ncompletely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\\\\\\\\nbranch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\\\\\\\\nchanges\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" — and, clarifying the nightly's target: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"it should not be for\\\\\\\\nonly a worktree/branch. the nightly complete test should be for master.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTwo tiers, by target and purpose:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **The nightly full suite runs MASTER ONLY** (`~/ci/nightly-full.sh` on\\\\\\\\n  shiva, tmux session `nightly`, scheduled 03:00 daily through the\\\\\\\\n  orchestrator's automation; it fetches and checks out origin/master\\\\\\\\n  before anything runs). Every lane, every host target: the JVM full\\\\\\\\n  suite, the full Node suite, the full Dart suite. Its results are a\\\\\\\\n  post-hoc integrity net for master, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\\\\\\\\n  finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\\\\\\\\n- **Branch/worktree testing runs only the namespaces that target the\\\\\\\\n  changed code** (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff\\\\\\\\n  touches), one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node)\\\\\\\\n  when cljc goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch:\\\\\\\\n  a branch gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree\\\\\\\\n  re-proven. A branch is never promoted by its own testing — the merge\\\\\\\\n  to master is what puts it under the nightly.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAn orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\\\\\\\\nsign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\\\\\\\\nnightly owns the follow-up.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\\\\\\\\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\\\\\\\\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\\\\\\\\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\\\\\\\\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\\\\\\\\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nFirst full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\\\\\\\\nANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\\\\\\\\nreplaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\\\\\\\\n(`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\\\\\\\\n**Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\\\\\\\\nlaptop — route Node lanes to the laptop or shiva); **Dart quick tier\\\\\\\\nrc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\\\\\\\\n(`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\\\\\\\\ntaken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\\\\\\\\nis the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\\\\\\\\ntime matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\\\\\\\\nmedians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOne exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\\\\\\\\n`gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\\\\\\\\nsame time — they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Patch does not apply\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" or \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".\\\\\\\\nRun them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\\\\\\\\nThe fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\\\\\\\\ngenerator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\\\\\\\\none week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOwner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs — `claude`, `codex`, `glm` —\\\\\\\\nare installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\\\\\\\\norchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"run tests remotely\\\\\\\\nover SSH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"delegate whole work agents remotely\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": a heavy implementation or\\\\\\\\nverification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\\\\\\\\nsuites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\\\\\\\\norchestrator's laptop unloaded.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\\\\\\\\nreference):\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. **Land the branch on origin first** (push from the laptop; never rsync) and\\\\\\\\n   prepare the scratch clone per \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Get the code there\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".\\\\\\\\n2. **Start a named tmux session** for the task; run the agent inside it with\\\\\\\\n   the toolchain activated *inside* the tmux command (`eval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s\\\\\\\\n   bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` after the `cd` — the non-interactive SSH shell does not have it).\\\\\\\\n   A fresh clone hits mise's interactive trust prompt, which blocks the whole\\\\\\\\n   session until answered via `tmux send-keys`; pre-empt it with `mise trust`\\\\\\\\n   inside the same command.\\\\\\\\n3. **The remote agent works in the clone**: implement, build, run lanes,\\\\\\\\n   write its findings artifact — the same brief form as any delegate dispatch\\\\\\\\n   (role template, bounded file ownership, deliverable path), just executing\\\\\\\\n   on the host. Long jobs survive SSH drops because of tmux.\\\\\\\\n4. **The orchestrator polls detached files, never the pane** (pane captures\\\\\\\\n   carry control characters that make output look empty): rc files, summary\\\\\\\\n   logs and `ls *.flag` sentinels written by the run.\\\\\\\\n5. **Division of labor**: bookkeeping and polling are orchestrator SSH\\\\\\\\n   one-liners; execution belongs to the remote agent. Do not run heavy lanes\\\\\\\\n   from the orchestrator's laptop while a remote agent owns the same gate.\\\\\\\\n6. **Same trust rules as any delegate**: remote execution does not waive\\\\\\\\n   Workflow steps 6–7 of the orchestrator role — artifacts are verified, and\\\\\\\\n   review before commit applies to remote-produced work identically.\\\\\\\\n   Session-ID capture and resume rules from the delegate-invocation reference\\\\\\\\n   apply per host (each host's `~/.claude`, `~/.codex` store is its own).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nKnown state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\\\\\\\\njolt canonical-fixes verification ran there); `glm` reported available by the\\\\\\\\nowner on both hosts, not yet exercised. Both hosts are toolchain-verified for\\\\\\\\nthe full tri-host suite (jupiter verified 2026-10-29 — see Measured times;\\\\\\\\nits 3.41.9 Flutter was replaced with 3.47.8, and model availability per\\\\\\\\nhost is governed by `routing-status.md`, not this file).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\\\\\\\\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\\\\\\\\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\\\\\\\\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\\\\\\\\n   ```sh\\\\\\\\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\\\\\\\\n   ```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\\\\\\\\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\\\\\\\\n   Common patterns:\\\\\\\\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\\\\\\\\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\\\\\\\\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\\\\\\\\nrg: scripts: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\\\\\\\nsrc/clj/yin/demo.clj:258:                ;; Transfer the store (holds the 'step closure) along with\\\\\\\\ntest/dao/star/r5_test.clj:2:  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Round r5: a grant deferred by an unusable reading is never established over\\\\\\\\ntest/dao/star/r5_test.clj:12:(deftest a-lapse-behind-a-deferred-grant-is-read-in-order\\\\\\\\ntest/yin/repl_test.cljc:135:            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"each program is its own segment; earlier closures still resolve\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")))\\\\\\\\ntest/yin/repl_test.cljc:181:        (is (nil? (:last-value state)) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"no closure outlives its code segment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\\ntest/yin/repl_test.cljc:541:            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"a closure from an earlier segment still resolves\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\\ntest/yin/repl_test.cljc:741:      (let [[_ [plus _ q nested alias _ closure]]\\\\\\\\ntest/yin/repl_test.cljc:756:        (is (str/starts-w